    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d515">

                <pb facs="0858" n="1816" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1816"/>

                <head>No. 60.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Garland</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-10-27"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 27,
                        1888</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to previous correspondence
                    concerning the disposition of the British vessels <hi rendition="#italic">Grace,
                        Dolphin</hi>, and <hi rendition="#italic">Anna Beck</hi>, condemned for
                    taking seals illicitly in Behring Sea, I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your letter of the 20th instant on the subject, and <pb facs="0859"
                        n="1817" xml:id="pg1817"/> to say in reply that, under the circumstances,
                    the wiser course for all concerned is to let the sale take place at once, as
                    delay is impracticable and can only be productive of loss.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
